when you were assigning those role-based permissions, how did you decide what access each person actually needed? Did you follow any specific methodology or principle?
Yes. While assigning access, I followed the principle of least privilege, which means users were given only the minimum access required to perform their job responsibilities. This helps reduce security risks and prevents unauthorized access to sensitive systems.
The process usually started with an approved access request from the manager or application owner. I reviewed the user’s role, department, and project requirements, and then matched those requirements with existing Active Directory security groups or predefined access templates used within the organization.
For example:
•	Finance users received access only to finance shared drives and applications 
•	Developers received UAT or development environment access 
•	Standard users received basic Office 365, VPN, and collaboration tool access 
Instead of assigning permissions directly to individual users, we mainly used group-based access control through Active Directory groups. This made access management easier, more secure, and easier to audit.
For sensitive systems such as production or database access, additional approvals and validation were required before granting permissions. In some cases, we also coordinated with security or application teams to ensure compliance with internal policies.
After assigning access, I verified functionality with the user and documented all changes in ServiceNow for tracking and audit purposes.
