I followed the principle of least privilege and role-based access control (RBAC). Access was granted based on the user’s job role, department, and approved business requirement. Most permissions were managed through AD security groups rather than direct assignment to ensure security, consistency, and easier auditing.
how do you handle access removal or deprovisioning when someone leaves the company or changes roles?
When handling access removal or deprovisioning, I follow a structured process to ensure security, compliance, and minimal business risk.
Typically, the process begins with an official request from HR, the manager, or the identity/access management team when an employee leaves the organization or changes roles. Once the request is verified, I immediately disable the user account in Active Directory or Azure AD to prevent unauthorized access.
For employees leaving the company, I:
•	Disable AD and Office 365 accounts 
•	Remove the user from security and distribution groups 
•	Revoke VPN, email, and application access 
•	Disable MFA or mobile authentication methods 
•	Deprovision access to shared drives, Citrix, and enterprise applications 
•	Coordinate with other teams if additional systems are involved 
For role changes or internal transfers, I follow role-based access control principles:
•	Remove old permissions that are no longer required 
•	Assign new access based on the updated role and approvals 
•	Validate access with the manager or application owner 
I also ensure all actions are documented properly in ServiceNow or the ticketing system for audit and compliance tracking. In high-security environments, timely deprovisioning is critical to reduce security risks and maintain compliance standards.
