One more question on this - when you're dealing with sync issues between Active Directory and Azure AD, what's your approach to figuring out what went wrong?
When troubleshooting sync issues between Active Directory and Azure AD, I usually follow a step-by-step approach to identify whether the issue is related to the on-prem AD object, Azure AD Connect synchronization, or permissions/configuration problems.
The first thing I do is verify the scope of the issue:
•	Is it affecting a single user or multiple users? 
•	Is the issue with password sync, account creation, group membership, or mailbox access? 
For a single-user issue, I typically start by checking:
•	Whether the account exists and is active in on-prem Active Directory 
•	OU placement and whether that OU is included in Azure AD Connect sync 
•	Correct UPN, proxy address, and attribute configuration 
•	Group memberships and licensing status in Azure AD 
Then I check the synchronization status using Azure AD Connect tools and PowerShell commands if needed. I review:
•	Synchronization Service Manager 
•	Export/import errors 
•	Connector health 
•	Last successful sync cycle 
Common issues I’ve encountered include:
•	Duplicate or conflicting attributes 
•	Incorrect UPN suffix 
•	Accounts placed in non-synced OUs 
•	Password hash sync delays 
•	Disabled or expired accounts 
If needed, I trigger a delta sync or full sync after correcting the issue and then validate whether the object appears correctly in Azure AD and Office 365.
For broader sync failures affecting multiple users, I would also:
•	Check Azure AD Connect service health 
•	Review server connectivity and authentication 
•	Validate sync scheduler status 
•	Escalate to the IAM or infrastructure team if deeper investigation is required 
Throughout the process, I document findings and updates in ServiceNow and keep users informed until resolution.
________________________________________
💡 Short Interview Version
My approach is to first identify whether the issue is isolated or widespread, then verify the AD object, OU sync scope, UPN attributes, and Azure AD Connect health. I review sync errors, connector status, and synchronization logs, correct any attribute or configuration issues, and trigger a sync cycle to validate resolution.
tell me about a time when you had to deal with a more complex Active Directory issue. Something that wasn't just a straightforward reset or sync problem.
Yes. One complex Active Directory issue I handled involved multiple users suddenly losing access to shared drives and certain enterprise applications after an internal departmental migration.
At first, it looked like a normal access issue, but after initial checks I noticed the users’ accounts were active, passwords were working, and VPN connectivity was normal. The problem was more widespread because several users from the same department were affected.
I started troubleshooting by reviewing:
•	AD group memberships 
•	Recently applied Group Policy changes 
•	OU movement history 
•	File share permissions 
•	Azure AD synchronization status 
During the investigation, I found that the affected users had been moved to a different Organizational Unit (OU) as part of a restructuring activity. The new OU had different Group Policy Objects (GPOs) applied, and some inherited security group mappings were no longer being applied correctly. As a result, users lost access to network drives and some application permissions tied to AD security groups.
To resolve the issue, I:
•	Compared affected users with working users 
•	Reviewed GPO inheritance and security filtering 
•	Re-added missing security group memberships 
•	Forced Group Policy updates using gpupdate 
•	Verified replication across domain controllers 
•	Coordinated with infrastructure and application teams to validate access restoration 
After changes were applied, I tested access with users and confirmed that shared drives, Outlook integrations, and business applications were functioning correctly again.
I also documented the root cause and recommended adding a validation checklist before future OU migrations to avoid similar issues. That helped improve the change process and reduced repeat incidents.
________________________________________
💡 Short Interview Version
I handled a complex AD issue where users lost application and shared drive access after being moved to a new OU. I investigated AD groups, GPO inheritance, and replication, identified missing inherited permissions, restored correct group memberships, forced policy updates, and validated access with users. I also documented the root cause and suggested process improvements to prevent recurrence.
