when you're looking at a device that's non-compliant, walk me through how you'd actually fix it. What steps would you take to get that device back into compliance?
When a device is marked as non-compliant in Intune, my approach is to first identify which compliance policy is failing and then guide the user through remediation in a structured way.
The first step is to check the device status in Microsoft Intune / Endpoint Manager and review:
•	Compliance policy details 
•	Error messages 
•	Last check-in status 
•	Affected settings or security requirements 
Common reasons I’ve seen for non-compliance include:
•	Outdated Windows version 
•	BitLocker not enabled 
•	Antivirus or Microsoft Defender disabled 
•	Missing security updates 
•	Device not checking in recently 
•	Password or PIN policy mismatch 
Once I identify the issue, I take corrective action based on the root cause.
For example:
•	If the OS version is outdated → I guide the user to run Windows Update and restart the device 
•	If BitLocker is disabled → I enable encryption and confirm policy sync 
•	If Defender or antivirus is inactive → I start required services and validate protection status 
•	If the device hasn’t synced recently → I manually trigger a sync from Company Portal or Intune 
After remediation, I:
1.	Force a device sync 
2.	Recheck compliance status in Intune 
3.	Validate user access to resources like Outlook, Teams, VPN, or SharePoint 
4.	Document the resolution in ServiceNow 
If the issue persists or appears policy-related, I coordinate with the Intune or security administration team to review compliance policies, conditional access settings, or deployment configurations.
My focus is always to restore compliance quickly while ensuring security standards are maintained.
